### Pull Request check-list

_Please make sure to review and check all of these items:_

- [x] Does `npm run test` or `npm run test-DIALECT` pass with this change (including linting)?
- [ ] Does the description below contain a link to an existing issue (Closes #[issue]) or a description of the issue you are solving?
- [ ] Have you added new tests to prevent regressions?
- [ ] Is a documentation update included (if this change modifies existing APIs, or introduces new ones)?
- [ ] Did you update the typescript typings accordingly (if applicable)?
- [x] Did you follow the commit message conventions explained in [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/sequelize/sequelize/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md)?

### Description of change

This replaces (almost) all `_.clone` calls with object spreading (there was 1 array clone).

Turns out `_.clone` is around least an order of magnitude slower compared to native object spreading!

Consider this (rather naive) performance test:

```js
'use strict';

const { clone } = require('lodash');

const data = {
  a: 1,
  b: 2,
  c: 3,
  d: 4,
  e: 5
};


let out;
console.time('clone');
for (let i = 0; i < 0xffffff; i++) {
  data[Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)] = Math.random(); //avoid opt
  out = clone(data);
}
console.timeEnd('clone');

console.time('spread');
for (let i = 0; i < 0xffffff; i++) {
  data[Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)] = Math.random(); //avoid opt
  out = { ...data };
}
console.timeEnd('spread');
console.log(out); // avoid opt
```

The results (on my machine):
```
$ node speed.js
clone: 23.944s
spread: 840.681ms
```

See also https://www.measurethat.net/Benchmarks/Show/4542/0/object-cloning-with-lodash-clone-vs-clonedeep-vs-merge
